        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Wall penetrations in utility room.



Below the baseboard along the enitre wall.



Dryer vent wall penetration very leaky.



Master Bedroom window is leaky aroung rough opening.



Back door showing lots of air leakage, need new weatherstripping.



Bedroom windows are very leaky and may need to be replaced.



Ceiing fixtures not sealed properly.



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>There is a few places in that attic that are missing insulation. This is causing unnecessary heat loss in these areas. The uninsulated areas should be filled with insulation. 



The ceiling fixtures are not sealed properly as well so this should be fixed befor adding any more insulation



An additional R-30 of insulation should be added over top of the exisiting layer as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.</ceilings>
                <windows>Replace 2 south facing windows.



Upgrade windows to energy star zone 3 rated.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on windows/doors and the criteria for these products.

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If there are air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as an HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew.



The HRV/ERV will recover heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. 



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Upgrade boiler to a condensing gas model with a 95% AFUE. A Combo Heateing/DHW Condensing boiler is a good option as well. This system will have direct supply/exhaust which will eliminate the need for the fresh supply air duct in the furnace room. This will decrease the amount of gas burned during the heating season and will reduce cold air entering the home. 



Install a digital or smart thermostat to help reduce fuel consumption.



Only cut off the supply for combustion air if both heating and hot water are direct vented/condensing.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on heating system upgrades and the criteria for these products.</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>Replace Conventional tank for an instantaneous on-demand DHW unit with EF of 0.95. A Combo heating/DHW condensing boiler is a good option as well.



Only cut off the supply for combustion air if both heating and hot water are direct vented/condensing.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on DHW upgrades and the criteria for these products.

</WaterConservation>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
